<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/advanced/bayesian-statistics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Bayesian statistics treats probability as <strong>degrees of belief that update with
    evidence</strong> — formally, via Bayes' theorem. Where classical testing asks "how
    surprising is this data if the null is true?", the Bayesian asks directly: "given this data,
    how believable is each hypothesis?" The price of that directness: you must state a
    <strong>prior</strong> — and defend it. Everything in this chapter is built from the
    probability grammar of the syllabus — notation, complements, the addition rule,
    independence, conditional probability and tree diagrams — because Bayes' theorem is not a
    new rule at all: it is what conditional probability does when you turn it around.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the theorem, the update loop, and the medical-test trap that saves lives to understand" widget="equationStep" config={bayesCfg} />

  <h2>Probability notation and the rules of combination</h2>
  <p>
    Fix the grammar first. P(A) is the probability of event A, a number in [0, 1]. The
    <strong>complement</strong> P(A') = 1 − P(A) — "the event does not happen" — which is
    often the cheapest route to an answer (the probability of at least one head in three flips
    is 1 − (½)³ = 0.875, easier than summing three cases). The <strong>addition rule</strong>
    says P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B): you subtract the overlap because counting both
    events counts their intersection twice. When events are <strong>mutually exclusive</strong>
    (both cannot happen — a card cannot be a heart and a spade), the overlap is empty and the
    rule collapses to P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B). Events are <strong>independent</strong> when one
    tells you nothing about the other, P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B) — and this is where replacement
    matters: drawing two aces from a 52-card deck <em>with</em> replacement gives (4/52)² ≈
    0.0059, but <em>without</em> replacement the second draw is conditional on the first, giving
    (4/52) × (3/51) ≈ 0.0045. Every real gambler's fallacy is a violation of one of these rules
    — usually treating dependent events as independent.
  </p>
  <p>
    The hinge of the whole subject is <strong>conditional probability</strong>: P(A|B) =
    P(A ∩ B) / P(B) — of the world where B happened, the fraction where A also happens. It is
    asymmetric by nature: P(rain|clouds) is high, P(clouds|rain) is 1. Tree diagrams make
    combined events visual and, crucially, get the conditioning direction right: each branch
    carries a conditional probability, each path's probability is the product along it
    (multiplication rule), and to find "the probability of reaching any leaf of type X" you sum
    the paths. Draw the medical-test scenario as a tree and the famous trap solves itself —
    which is the next section.
  </p>

  <h2>Bayes' theorem, derived rather than announced</h2>
  <p>
    Conditional probability gives two ways to write the same joint event: P(A ∩ B) =
    P(A|B)·P(B) and also P(A ∩ B) = P(B|A)·P(A). Set them equal and divide by P(B):
  </p>
  <p>
    P(A|B) = P(B|A) · P(A) / P(B).
  </p>
  <p>
    That is Bayes' theorem — one line of algebra, and the whole of Bayesian statistics. In the
    hypothesis reading: P(A|B) is the <strong>posterior</strong> (belief in hypothesis A after
    seeing evidence B); P(B|A) is the <strong>likelihood</strong> (how strongly hypothesis A
    predicts the evidence); P(A) is the <strong>prior</strong> (belief before the evidence);
    and P(B) is a normalising constant (the total probability of the evidence under all
    hypotheses). The theorem is nothing more than the multiplication rule read backwards — but
    that backwards reading is exactly the step human intuition refuses to take unaided, which
    is why the theorem has a name and a anniversary.
  </p>

  <h2>The medical test trap, with numbers</h2>
  <p>
    A disease affects 1 in 10,000 people. A test catches 99% of true cases (sensitivity 0.99)
    and clears 99% of healthy people (specificity 0.99). You test positive. What is the
    probability you are ill? Intuition shouts "99%". Take a concrete population of 1,000,000
    (this is the tree diagram drawn with people): about <strong>100 are ill</strong>, and the
    test flags 99 of them. The <strong>999,900 healthy</strong> people produce false positives
    at 1%: about <strong>9,999</strong> healthy people flagged. Total flagged: 10,098. The
    probability of illness given a positive test is the true positives over all positives:
    99 / 10,098 ≈ <strong>0.98%</strong>. You are probably healthy — a positive result on a
    "99%-accurate" test for a rare disease is right about 1 time in 100. The mechanism is not
    statistics being clever; it is the base rate: because illness is so rare, even a thin
    1% error rate on the enormous healthy population drowns the near-complete catch of the tiny
    ill population. In likelihood-ratio form: posterior odds = prior odds × 99, and prior odds
    were 1 : 9,999.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Bayesian engine also shows the cure: <em>repeat the test</em>. Make yesterday's
    posterior today's prior — odds 1 : 101 after the first positive — and a second independent
    positive multiplies by 99 again: odds ≈ 1 : 1.02, so the probability of illness jumps to
    about <strong>49.5%</strong>. A third positive takes it to roughly <strong>99%</strong>.
    Sequential updating — evidence accumulating on evidence, each round's posterior feeding the
    next prior — is the qualitative behaviour of all scientific inference, and this little
    table (0.98% → 49.5% → 99%) is the best one-page demonstration of it in the subject.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a Bayesian prior is just an opinion that contaminates the
    analysis." A prior is an explicit statement of what was known before the data — previous
    studies, physical constraints, the base rate itself. Its influence shrinks as data
    accumulates (the likelihood dominates eventually), and it is exposed to attack precisely
    because it is written down — a frequentist's hidden assumptions are harder to interrogate
    than a Bayesian's prior. The real sin is an unstated assumption, not a stated one.
  </p>

  <h2>Priors, likelihoods and the update loop in practice</h2>
  <p>
    The general machinery: choose a prior distribution over the unknown, multiply by the
    likelihood of the observed data, normalise, and read off the posterior — "prior ×
    likelihood ∝ posterior". A coin-flip example with real numbers makes the prior's role
    visible. Unknown: the coin's head probability θ. Uniform prior (every θ between 0 and 1
    equally believable — the Beta(1,1) distribution). Data: 7 heads in 10 flips. The posterior
    is then Beta(8, 4), whose mean is 8/12 ≈ 0.67 — close to the raw 0.7, pulled slightly
    toward 0.5. Now the informative prior: a mint inspector tells you coins run true, encoded
    as Beta(2,2). The same 7-in-10 data gives posterior Beta(9, 4), mean 9/13 ≈ 0.69 — the
    prior is a gentle nudge, already fading. Feed in 7,000 heads in 10,000 flips and no
    reasonable prior matters at all: the likelihood has spoken. The sensitivity analysis this
    invites — redo the analysis under several defensible priors and report whether conclusions
    move — is standard practice and the honest answer to "aren't priors subjective?".
    Everyday Bayes runs the same loop invisibly: spam filters multiply per-word likelihood
    ratios into a spam probability and act on a threshold; a doctor reading a mammogram is
    doing posterior odds with an age-dependent prior; and the "suspicious package" judgments
    of a seasoned security officer are priors updated by smells, wires and behaviour.
  </p>

  <h2>Bayes in the courtroom: the prosecutor's fallacy</h2>
  <p>
    The medical trap has a twin with liberty at stake. A DNA profile from a crime scene matches
    the defendant, and an expert testifies the match probability is 1 in a million. The
    prosecutor's fallacy is reading that as "the probability the defendant is innocent is 1 in a
    million". What the 1-in-a-million is, is the <em>likelihood</em> — P(match | innocent,
    random person). The posterior — P(innocent | match) — needs the prior: the chance the
    guilty party was this person at all. In a country of 60 million, about 60 people are
    expected to share that profile; if there is no other evidence, the probability of guilt
    given the match is roughly 1/60 ≈ 1.7%, not 99.9999%. The structure is exactly the medical
    test: a rare event (guilt), a high-sensitivity test (the profile), and a tiny per-person
    false-positive rate that still produces dozens of matches across a large population.
    Courts and expert-witness guidance now formalise likelihood ratios — evidence multiplies
    the prior odds, and the jury's job is the prior — precisely because this fallacy has
    contributed to real wrongful convictions. Same mathematics, higher stakes.
  </p>

  <h2>Decision theory: what the posterior is <em>for</em></h2>
  <p>
    The posterior distribution is not the end of the analysis; it is the input to a decision.
    Bayesian decision theory makes this explicit: attach a loss function to each action under
    each state of the world, and choose the action that minimises expected loss, where the
    expectation is taken over the posterior. The medical example shows why this matters more
    than the probability alone. A 0.98% posterior chance of illness sounds dismissible — until
    you price the actions: the loss of missing a treatable disease is enormous relative to the
    loss of a follow-up scan, so the optimal decision at a 1% probability may still be "scan
    anyway". Different losses flip the decision at the same posterior: an airport security
    officer and an oncologist can rationally act differently on identical evidence because
    their loss tables differ. This framework also names the threshold behaviour every applied
    system exhibits: spam filters, fraud engines and fire alarms all compare a posterior (or a
    likelihood-ratio transform of one) against a decision threshold, and setting that threshold
    is an explicit trade between false alarms and misses — a trade the mathematics cannot make
    for you, because it requires valuing outcomes, not just believing hypotheses.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Conjugacy and why the Beta example was tidy.</strong> The Beta family is the
    <em>conjugate</em> prior for the binomial likelihood: prior Beta(a, b) plus k successes in
    n trials gives posterior Beta(a + k, b + n − k), exactly — no integration. Conjugate pairs
    (Beta–Binomial, Gamma–Poisson, Normal–Normal) carried Bayesian computation for decades;
    modern problems are not so kind, and the posterior of a neural network or a hierarchical
    model has no closed form. The solution is <strong>Markov chain Monte Carlo</strong>
    (MCMC): construct a random walk whose long-run visits follow the posterior distribution —
    the computer cannot compute the normalising constant, but it does not need to, since the
    unnormalised "prior × likelihood" surface is enough to walk on. Tools like Stan and PyMC
    turned this from specialist craft into routine practice, which is the real reason the
    Bayesian revival happened after 1990: the mathematics was old, the computation was new.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What the posterior gives you that a p-value does not.</strong> A 95% credible
    interval means "given the data and prior, the parameter lies in this range with probability
    0.95" — a statement you can actually use. A 95% confidence interval means "the procedure
    that produced this interval captures the true value 95% of the time across repeated
    experiments" — a statement about hypothetical repetitions, silent about this interval. The
    Bayesian framework also handles <em>sequential</em> analysis naturally (update and stop
    whenever, no p-hacking, since each interim conclusion is a valid posterior), which
    frequentist testing requires corrections for. <strong>Open questions:</strong> objective
    versus subjective priors (Jeffreys priors, reference analysis — is there a prior that adds
    no information?); Bayes factors and their sensitivity to prior width; approximate Bayesian
    computation for simulator models where the likelihood is unavailable; and the deep-learning
    frontier, where "the prior" is the architecture and training data, and principled
    uncertainty quantification over billions of parameters remains unsolved. The base-rate trap,
    meanwhile, never retired: every screening programme, fraud alert and anomaly detector is a
    Bayes computation, and every operator who skips the prior is about to call 9,999 healthy
    people sick.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bayes (1763); Laplace, Théorie analytique des probabilités; McElreath, Statistical Rethinking; Gelman et al., Bayesian Data Analysis.</p>
